  "prediction": "**Wildlife Trafficking: FWS and NOAA's Use of Financial Rewards**\n\nThe U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (FWS) and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) have laws that authorize them to pay financial rewards for information on wildlife trafficking. However, our review found that FWS and NOAA have not prioritized the use of financial rewards as a tool to combat wildlife trafficking.\n\nWe identified 11 laws that FWS and NOAA use to pay rewards for information on wildlife trafficking, including the Endangered Species Act and the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act. However, FWS and NOAA reported paying only 27 rewards from fiscal years 2007 to 2017, with FWS paying 25 rewards and NOAA paying 2 rewards.\n\nOur review also found that FWS and NOAA have policies and procedures in place to track and manage financial rewards, but they do not have a comprehensive system to track and analyze the effectiveness of these rewards. We recommended that FWS and NOAA track financial reward information clearly and make it readily available for examination.\n\nIn addition, we found that FWS and NOAA have not communicated effectively with the public about the availability of financial rewards for information on wildlife trafficking. We recommended that FWS and NOAA develop a plan to communicate more effectively with the public about the availability of financial rewards and the process for submitting tips.\n\nOur review also identified opportunities for FWS and NOAA to improve their use of financial rewards as a tool to combat wildlife trafficking. We recommended that FWS and NOAA review the effectiveness of their financial reward programs and consider adjusting their policies and procedures to better leverage these rewards.\n\nOverall, our review found that FWS and NOAA have the authority to pay financial rewards for information on wildlife trafficking, but they have not prioritized the use of these rewards as a tool to combat this crime. By implementing our recommendations, FWS and NOAA can improve their use of financial rewards and better combat wildlife trafficking.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. FWS and NOAA should track financial reward information clearly and make it readily available for examination.\n2. FWS and NOAA should develop a plan to communicate more effectively with the public about the availability of financial rewards and the process for submitting tips.\n3. FWS and NOAA should review the effectiveness of their financial reward programs and consider adjusting their policies and procedures to better leverage these rewards.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nWildlife trafficking is a significant threat to the environment and human security, and FWS and NOAA have a critical role to play in combating this crime.",
